1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Daniel Jonsson Behmer=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Jon Mickelsson Behm and Anna Danielsdotter Kröger
2 CONT *'''Birth:''' October 01, 1611 in Gävleborg, Sweden
2 CONT * '''Death:'''	December 20, 1669 (58) in Älvkarleby, Uppsala län, Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/18414#/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/18415 Daniel Behmer, Lawyer, Civil Servant]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Behmer, before the nobility Daniel Jonæ Gewalius , later Gislenius (Gislenus, Gissel) and finally, from 1638, Behm, b. Oct. 1611 in Gävle, d. Dec. 29, 1669 in Älvkarleby. Parents: mayor John Mickelsson and Anna Danielsdotte
2 CONC r Kröger. Enjoyed school education in Gävle; student in Uppsala 1 Feb. 1626; disp. 18 June 1631 (Disputationum de usu logices secunda, legitimum logicæ in SS theologiâ usum ostendens generatim; pres. L. Stigzelius, resp. Dan. Jonæ
2 CONC  Gewalius) and 29 Apr. 1634 (De habitibus, ut in anima sunt, generatim & communibus nonullis eorum divisionibus; pres. MO Stenius, resp. Dan. Jonæ Gislenus); informant for Axel Oxenstierna's son Erik June 1634–1638; undertook a fo
2 CONC reign trip 1 Aug. 1638–Nov. 1640. Professor of eloquence in Uppsala 16 Nov. 1640; Secretary of the Crown 14 Jan. 1645; District Governor of Långhundra and Tuhundra District 3 Jan. 1646; Assessor in the Svea Court of Appeal 17 Apr.
2 CONC  1649; knighted 15 June 1650; Assessor of the second class 23 Nov. 1654; State Secretary Apr. 1655; also Commissioner in the Reduction Board 1655–62; Councilor of the Court 13 Dec. 1664; member of the Lower Court of Justice with t
2 CONC he task of presiding there in the absence of the Chancellor of the Court 22 Nov. 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Married 1) 26 July 1643 to Brita Schütte, b. 10 Nov. 1623, d. 18 May 1668, daughter of the alderman and merchant in Nyköping Henning Schütte; 2) 24 Apr. 1669 with Magdalena Lindegren, b. 18 June 1646, daughter of the assessor i
2 CONC n the Göta Court of Appeal Nils Lindegren and remarried to the cavalry master Karl Gustav Lood.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT During his many years of study at Uppsala University, B. made himself known and respected through his learning and his interest in study; Johan Oxenstierna wrote of him in 1636 that "he is a young servant, who is difficult to inst
2 CONC ruct" and "walks with good ease", and Johan Salvius gave him the grade a few years later that he was the most learned Uppsala student he had ever known. It is not surprising that Axel Oxenstierna , who devoted the greatest care to
2 CONC  the upbringing of his sons, appointed him as tutor for his younger son Erik., who came to Uppsala as a nine-year-old boy to receive education. In this capacity, B. worked for four years (1634-38), apparently with success and to h
2 CONC is father's satisfaction. The salary was admittedly not large (150 dirhams per year, it is stated), but in return B. came to enter into a close personal relationship with the Oxenstierna family during this activity, which in many 
2 CONC ways was useful to him for the future. Thus, it was essentially the Chancellor's support that gave B. the opportunity to embark on a foreign trip in 1638, which, apparently thanks to additional contributions during the trip, was e
2 CONC xtended over two years. The trip was to Denmark, Holland, Belgium, England, France, Italy, Switzerland and Germany; Several foreign universities were visited, and the traveler took in with great curiosity everything of interest he
2 CONC  saw, and the company of all the distinguished persons he had the opportunity to meet thanks to his recommendations. Shortly after his return home, B. was appointed professor eloquentiæ at Uppsala University (1640). His work in th
2 CONC is position was successful and respected: his lectures and examinations were unusually well attended, and he was considered for the position of rector of the university, although he refused to accept the election for formal reason
2 CONC s (1641). He strongly held the old university's monopoly position in learned studies. When the university's chancellor Johan Skytte, during his visit to Uppsala in 1644, demanded that some students travel to Stockholm to pursue me
2 CONC dical studies with the French physician GF Du Rietz, B. opposed it energetically; he had the matter postponed until further notice and in the meantime appealed to the university chancellor to the chancellor to prevent such a handi
2 CONC ng over of Swedish students to a "heretical" teacher. Like many other learned men of the time, B. was soon drawn from academic to administrative work. In 1645 he became secretary in the chancellery and was then, within a couple of
2 CONC  decades, appointed assessor in the Svea Court of Appeal, state secretary, commissioner in the reduction board, councilor of the court and member of the lower justices' audit. In addition, B. was from time to time given all sorts 
2 CONC of temporary assignments. Thus, in 1646 he was sent to his native city of Gävle to discuss with its mayor, council and citizens the upliftment and improvement of the city through trade, crafts, fishing, hop farms, construction act
2 CONC ivities, the "discipline" of youth, etc.; he returned with the request of the people of Gävle that they should regain their free navigation, which they had been partially deprived of through the introduction of the "Botnian trade 
2 CONC embargo". In the autumn of the same year he was on a journey further up North, during which he visited Härnösand, Sundsvall and Hudiksvall with the surrounding countryside. During this he held discussions with the local authoritie
2 CONC s, during which the necessity of more rational forest management was emphasized; the towns found B. "through numerous disorders so confused and disorganized,that nothing can be said about speed", but he also showed an open mind fo
2 CONC r measures that could bring them out of their decline. A few years later (1648), B. participated, together with the two first governors of Gotland, Åke Ulfsparre and Gustav Banér, in a commission whose task was to settle some disp
2 CONC utes and bring order and prosperity to the island that had just come to Sweden. B. was still on intimate terms with the Oxenstierna; he was thus allowed to serve as an intermediary in the rapprochement between Axel Oxenstierna and
2 CONC  the royal princePer Brahe , as well as at the beginning of marriage negotiations between Erik Oxenstierna and Elsa Elisabet Brahe (1646). If Axel Oxenstierna needed advice on a teacher or tutor for one of his many protégés, he tu
2 CONC rned to B., just as on the other hand the latter confided to his powerful benefactor his concerns about unpaid wages or sought his mature advice when it came to complicated official procedures. During the long periods when the old
2 CONC  Chancellor was away from business in the country, B. was one of those who most diligently supplied him with news: political intelligence, appointments, descriptions of court festivities, etc. B. also had a similar confidential co
2 CONC rrespondence with Erik Oxenstierna, who succeeded his father in his office in 1654; he did not even hesitate to criticize the government's policy in all calmness, saying that it was his opinion and that of "many honest patriots" t
2 CONC hat the king should reconcile with the Polish king and that both should turn against Russia with united forces. B. seems to have been particularly well-regarded by Charles X Gustav; he had orders to write to him every week during 
2 CONC the king's stay in Poland and Denmark and seems to have occasionally expressed himself quite unreservedly about the government's measures in his homeland. B.'s financial position must have been generally quite worrying. He laments
2 CONC  that he was more disadvantaged than any of his peers in terms of donations; he had not been able to afford to acquire any property, and the salary from his various offices was occasionally reduced by half. However, he later subsc
2 CONC ribed to the properties Ytterbynäs and Tuppen in Skuttunge, of which the former had been purchased from the crown, while Tuppen had previously belonged to Axel Oxenstierna. Through Erik Oxenstierna's premature death (1656) he lost
2 CONC  a support from which he had expected much for his and his family's future. B. had already suffered from illness at an early age; in 1667 he suffered a stroke, and in 1669 he died suddenly in Älvkarleby during a journey that he an
2 CONC d his wife intended to undertake to Norrland. On 14 Aug. 1670 he was buried in Riddarholmskyrkan, after which the body was moved to Skuttunge church in Uppland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
